Do not lay out fixed/sticky renderers again on every asynchronously-handled scroll.

LocalFrameView::updateLayoutViewport() recomputes the layout viewport origin on
every scroll position change and, when it moved, calls setBaseLayoutViewportOrigin()
with the default TriggerLayoutOrNot::Yes -- which runs
setViewportConstrainedObjectsNeedLayout(), i.e. setNeedsLayout() on every
position:fixed and position:sticky renderer in the frame.

AsyncScrollingCoordinator::reconcileScrollingState() already pre-sets the same
origin with TriggerLayoutOrNot::No precisely so this does not happen, but the
origin it carries is the one the scrolling thread computed for the PREVIOUS
committed position, so on a continuous drag the recomputation here almost always
differs and the early return in setBaseLayoutViewportOrigin() does not fire.

Measured on a msm8998 phone (webkitgtk 2.52.6, GTK4/Wayland, async scrolling on),
one 11.7 s continuous drag of a settled 74 000 px article:

    setViewportConstrainedObjectsNeedLayout   540 calls
    AsyncScrollingCoordinator::reconcile      596 calls
    LocalFrameViewLayoutContext::performLayout 297 calls, 1565 ms

A fixed or sticky element's containing block is the RenderView, so one
setNeedsLayout() on it dirties the root: the next layout the page's own script
forces (any window.scrollY / offsetTop / scrollTop read in a scroll handler) is
then a document-wide layout instead of a no-op. The same article with its
scripts removed -- nothing forcing layout -- costs 9 layouts, which is why this
never showed up as "layout is hot" in a profile.

The condition under which this work changes nothing on screen is exactly the one
LocalFrameView::shouldUpdateCompositingLayersAfterScrolling() already computes
for the sibling call in updateCompositingLayersAfterScrolling(), and its comment
states the rule: "If the scrolling thread is updating the fixed elements, then
the LocalFrameView should not update them as well." It returns true -- keeping
the old behaviour -- for subframes, for programmatic scrolls, when there is no
scrolling coordinator, when the frame has synchronous scrolling reasons (which
includes HasNonLayerViewportConstrainedObjects, i.e. a fixed element that failed
to get a layer), and for anchor-positioned viewport-constrained objects. So the
layout is skipped only where the scrolling tree owns those layers' positions.

--- a/Source/WebCore/page/LocalFrameView.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/page/LocalFrameView.cpp
@@ -2006,7 +2006,14 @@ void LocalFrameView::updateLayoutViewport()
 
     LayoutPoint newLayoutViewportOrigin = computeLayoutViewportOrigin(visualViewportRect(), minStableLayoutViewportOrigin(), maxStableLayoutViewportOrigin(), layoutViewport, scrollBehaviorForFixedElements());
     if (newLayoutViewportOrigin != m_layoutViewportOrigin) {
-        setBaseLayoutViewportOrigin(newLayoutViewportOrigin);
+        // When the scrolling thread owns the viewport-constrained layers, laying their
+        // renderers out again on every scroll changes nothing on screen -- the same
+        // condition updateCompositingLayersAfterScrolling() uses to skip its own work.
+        // It is not free: a fixed or sticky element's containing block is the RenderView,
+        // so one setNeedsLayout() per scroll turns the next layout the page forces into a
+        // document-wide one.
+        setBaseLayoutViewportOrigin(newLayoutViewportOrigin, shouldUpdateCompositingLayersAfterScrolling()
+            ? TriggerLayoutOrNot::Yes : TriggerLayoutOrNot::No);
         LOG_WITH_STREAM(Scrolling, stream << "layoutViewport changed to " << layoutViewportRect());
     }
     layoutOrVisualViewportChanged();
